Void Pointer (Generic Pointer)

Void pointer = a pointer which can point to any type of address and is of void type.

Properties

When we are dealing with one address but don't know what type that address is → use void pointer.
We can store any type of address in a void pointer.
Void pointer is a generic pointer — can point to any type of address.
We cannot apply indirection (*) on void pointer directly.
Allowed operations on void pointer: Addition, Subtraction, Increment, Decrement (*, ++, --, -3).
If we want to apply these operations to a specific type, we need to cast first.

cint *test() {
    int a = 5;
    return &a;     // returning address of local → dangling pointer risk
}

void main() {
    int a = 10;
    void *vp = &a;       // void pointer pointing to int address
    printf("%d", *vp);   // CE: cannot dereference void pointer directly
    printf("%d", *(int*)vp);   // Cast first → 10
}
Void pointer to int cast:
cint *test() {
    int a = 5;
    return &a;
}
void main() {
    int a;
    a = test();        // warning: not returning same
    printf("%d", *a);  // O/P: GIV (dangling)
}
// O/P: CE / undefined
